The engineering stress-strain curves and related quantitative data of the designed steel obtained from different ausforming processes are presented in  Fig. 7 
                      a. As it is clear from the figure, all tensile curves are characterized by the presence of continuous yielding and uniform plastic deformation phenomenon, and a continuous work hardening can be observed. It is significant that ausforming process simultaneously brings about an increase in yield strength (YS) and ultimate tensile strength (UTS), as well as a greater range of tensile strain before fracture, compared to direct isothermal transformation (non-ausforming sample). Meanwhile, the comprehensive mechanical properties display an obviously increasing trend with increasing ausforming strain. More importantly, the sample subjected to ausforming for 40% strain demonstrates excellent tensile properties, with a YS of 1500 MPa, UTS of 1900 MPa and uniform elongation of 24.4%, gives a maximum product of strength and elongation (PSE) about 46.4 GPa·%, which testifies that the excellent tensile performance can be achieved.  Fig. 7 b shows the increment of strength in different tensile stage. The non-ausforming sample exhibits the maximum increment in the range of yield stage to 3% tensile strain, and with increasing ausforming strain, the increment value ( Δσ 1 
                      ) gradually reduces. Nerveless, from 3% tensile strain to tensile fracture, there is a remarkable tendency for the increment values ( Δσ 2 
                      ) to enhance with increasing ausforming strain.

It has been proved that retained austenite volume fraction, morphology and its mechanical stability are all major parameters controlling the ductility properties  [45–47] . As is commonly known, the austenite-to-martensite transformation occurs within the uniform plastic deformation regime delaying the beginning of necking behavior and improving tensile strength. Previous studies demonstrate that undesirable blocky retained austenite, especially in central region, will be the preferred locations of transformation-induced martensite at a relatively small tensile strain range as a result of its low mechanical stability, when the micro local stress suppresses the critical value required to trigger martensite transformation  [48] . With decreasing ausforming strain, more quantity of large-scale blocky retained austenite can be observed in  Fig. 3 , which can transform into martensite at the initial deformation stage (from yield stage to 3% tensile strain) resulting in the more significant increment in strength ( Δσ 1 
                            ), as shown in  Fig. 7 b. However, this premature consumption will bring about unnecessarily rapid work hardening and not be favorable for being effective in providing a proficient transformation induced plasticity (TRIP) effect, leading to the reduced increment value ( Δσ 2 
                            ) at subsequent tensile process and early failure. Considerable research points out that the filmy retained austenite with nano-scale transforms into martensite in a progressive manner, which is essential for fully leveraging the TIRP effect upon plastic deformation to delay crack initiation, crack propagation and plastic instability, enabling the enhanced strain hardening behavior and a substantial increment in tensile properties  [48,49] . This is the reason that non-ausforming sample fractures prematurely at 16% elongation, whereas ausforming samples exhibit long uniform elongation. Therefore, it is favorable to diminish detrimental unstable blocky retained austenite and replace by beneficial filmy retained austenite to accommodate plastic deformation at large strain regime and achieve transformation toughening.
